1
00:00:00,200 --> 00:00:03,350
A drone slips toward a power substation at dusk.

2
00:00:03,350 --> 00:00:04,622
No radar sees it.

3
00:00:04,622 --> 00:00:06,197
No camera sees it yet.

4
00:00:06,197 --> 00:00:09,771
But the six-G base station beside the fence does, using

5
00:00:09,771 --> 00:00:12,860
nothing but the signal it already uses to talk.

6
00:00:13,660 --> 00:00:15,920
Critical sites have a blind spot:

7
00:00:15,920 --> 00:00:17,691
small, low-flying drones.

8
00:00:17,691 --> 00:00:19,706
Six-G proposes something new.

9
00:00:19,706 --> 00:00:24,958
Integrated sensing and communication, where the network itself becomes the sensor.

10
00:00:24,958 --> 00:00:28,012
It is not standardized yet, so we simulate it,

11
00:00:28,012 --> 00:00:31,920
because real hardware tests are costly or simply impossible.

12
00:00:32,720 --> 00:00:37,103
Traditional networks only communicate, and radar only senses.

13
00:00:37,103 --> 00:00:42,430
Integrated sensing and communication reuses the same radio signal for both.

14
00:00:42,430 --> 00:00:47,150
The base station already transmits wide-bandwidth O F D M signals.

15
00:00:47,150 --> 00:00:50,252
The echoes that bounce off a moving object

16
00:00:50,252 --> 00:00:53,556
carry its range, its speed and its direction,

17
00:00:53,556 --> 00:00:56,860
with no extra spectrum and no extra hardware.

18
00:00:57,660 --> 00:00:59,470
Here is how it is built.

19
00:00:59,470 --> 00:01:03,218
A ray-tracing model of the substation runs at thirteen

20
00:01:03,218 --> 00:01:07,096
gigahertz, with a four-hundred-megahertz O F D M signal.

21
00:01:07,096 --> 00:01:11,684
Sionna computes the clutter from buildings, transformers and fence.

22
00:01:11,684 --> 00:01:14,463
The drone is a moving point target of a

23
00:01:14,463 --> 00:01:18,599
hundred square centimetres, flying twelve metres per second.

24
00:01:18,599 --> 00:01:21,960
The whole simulation runs on one AWS L four GPU.

25
00:01:22,760 --> 00:01:23,649
The numbers.

26
00:01:23,649 --> 00:01:26,594
At thirteen gigahertz, in the upper mid band that

27
00:01:26,594 --> 00:01:29,650
six-G is expected to use, four hundred megahertz of

28
00:01:29,650 --> 00:01:33,706
bandwidth gives a range resolution of about thirty-eight centimetres.

29
00:01:33,706 --> 00:01:36,706
One thousand twenty-four subcarriers set a maximum

30
00:01:36,706 --> 00:01:39,262
range of three hundred eighty-four metres.

31
00:01:39,262 --> 00:01:42,317
A frame of two thousand forty-eight symbols gives a

32
00:01:42,317 --> 00:01:45,540
speed resolution of one point eight metres per second.

33
00:01:46,340 --> 00:01:49,477
The scene is a substation modelled in Sionna:

34
00:01:49,477 --> 00:01:54,021
concrete buildings, steel transformers, gantries and a metal fence.

35
00:01:54,021 --> 00:01:57,286
Ray tracing finds fifteen strong clutter paths.

36
00:01:57,286 --> 00:02:00,423
They are huge compared to the drone, but they

37
00:02:00,423 --> 00:02:03,815
do not move, so subtracting the average over time

38
00:02:03,815 --> 00:02:07,400
removes them, and what remains is the moving target.

39
00:02:08,200 --> 00:02:12,857
The base station removes the static clutter and forms a range-Doppler map.

40
00:02:12,857 --> 00:02:16,081
A C F A R detector flags anything above the noise.

41
00:02:16,081 --> 00:02:19,485
A sixteen by eight antenna panel estimates direction,

42
00:02:19,485 --> 00:02:22,052
and a Kalman tracker follows the drone.

43
00:02:22,052 --> 00:02:25,276
The track is confirmed at three hundred twenty-one

44
00:02:25,276 --> 00:02:28,620
metres, about twenty-seven seconds before the fence.

45
00:02:29,420 --> 00:02:30,426
About C F A R.

46
00:02:30,426 --> 00:02:33,724
The detector compares each cell with the average of its

47
00:02:33,724 --> 00:02:37,357
neighbours, and a threshold factor sets the false-alarm rate.

48
00:02:37,357 --> 00:02:40,095
We designed it for one in a million per cell.

49
00:02:40,095 --> 00:02:43,505
Measured on two hundred million noise-only cells, it came

50
00:02:43,505 --> 00:02:45,684
out at one point four in a million.

51
00:02:45,684 --> 00:02:50,379
That is slightly above design, because the window correlates neighbouring cells.

52
00:02:50,379 --> 00:02:52,000
We report it as measured.

53
00:02:52,800 --> 00:02:56,256
Direction comes from a sixteen by eight antenna panel.

54
00:02:56,256 --> 00:02:59,175
The drone must be seen in three frames before

55
00:02:59,175 --> 00:03:02,691
the track is confirmed, which filters out false alarms.

56
00:03:02,691 --> 00:03:06,623
A Kalman filter then predicts and corrects position and speed.

57
00:03:06,623 --> 00:03:09,900
The error keeps shrinking as the drone gets closer.

58
00:03:10,700 --> 00:03:13,614
Across twenty simulated flights, position error is

59
00:03:13,614 --> 00:03:15,935
sixty-four centimetres R M S, and speed

60
00:03:15,935 --> 00:03:18,094
error is under one metre per second.

61
00:03:18,094 --> 00:03:21,764
A target ten times smaller is still confirmed at the same range.

62
00:03:21,764 --> 00:03:25,218
At three hundred metres, the smallest target detected ninety

63
00:03:25,218 --> 00:03:28,240
percent of the time is about ten square centimetres.

64
00:03:29,040 --> 00:03:30,491
Target size matters.

65
00:03:30,491 --> 00:03:34,059
The smallest target detected ninety percent of the time

66
00:03:34,059 --> 00:03:37,627
at three hundred metres is about six square centimetres

67
00:03:37,627 --> 00:03:40,771
at a false-alarm setting of one in ten thousand,

68
00:03:40,771 --> 00:03:43,855
and ten square centimetres at one in a million.

69
00:03:43,855 --> 00:03:46,636
Birds in the same size range would also be

70
00:03:46,636 --> 00:03:49,539
detected, so telling them apart is a job for

71
00:03:49,539 --> 00:03:52,260
the agent and its rules, and a next step.

72
00:03:53,060 --> 00:03:54,890
Accuracy depends on range.

73
00:03:54,890 --> 00:03:58,122
At three hundred metres the median error is about

74
00:03:58,122 --> 00:04:01,354
one metre for the standard drone, and about three

75
00:04:01,354 --> 00:04:03,916
metres for a target ten times smaller.

76
00:04:03,916 --> 00:04:07,880
Inside one hundred metres, both are under twenty centimetres.

77
00:04:08,680 --> 00:04:10,321
Now the governed agent.

78
00:04:10,321 --> 00:04:14,819
When the track is confirmed, deterministic tools compute the evidence:

79
00:04:14,819 --> 00:04:19,621
distance, zone, time to the fence, and whether a registered flight matches.

80
00:04:19,621 --> 00:04:24,241
Then Meta's Llama three point one proposes actions from a fixed catalog.

81
00:04:24,241 --> 00:04:25,700
It never acts alone.

82
00:04:26,500 --> 00:04:28,515
What Llama actually sees.

83
00:04:28,515 --> 00:04:32,405
Not raw sensor data, only a compact evidence record:

84
00:04:32,405 --> 00:04:36,086
distance to the facility, altitude, ground speed,

85
00:04:36,086 --> 00:04:39,143
closing speed, seconds to the fence, the

86
00:04:39,143 --> 00:04:42,755
zone, and whether any registered flight matches.

87
00:04:42,755 --> 00:04:45,603
It also gets the policy rules and the

88
00:04:45,603 --> 00:04:49,424
action catalog, and must answer in structured data:

89
00:04:49,424 --> 00:04:53,800
a classification, a threat level, a rationale, and actions.

90
00:04:54,600 --> 00:04:57,020
A policy engine checks every proposal.

91
00:04:57,020 --> 00:04:59,556
It raises the threat level to the policy

92
00:04:59,556 --> 00:05:02,438
floor, adds the minimum actions for each zone,

93
00:05:02,438 --> 00:05:04,973
and blocks anything outside the catalog.

94
00:05:04,973 --> 00:05:06,818
Jamming is never executable.

95
00:05:06,818 --> 00:05:10,218
Actions that touch the physical world wait for a human.

96
00:05:10,218 --> 00:05:14,540
The duty officer approves the camera in three seconds, then the patrol.

97
00:05:15,340 --> 00:05:19,576
The security manager rejects an early police escalation, and approves

98
00:05:19,576 --> 00:05:22,187
it only when the drone is over the fence.

99
00:05:22,187 --> 00:05:25,320
The dashboard lets you play the operator yourself.

100
00:05:26,120 --> 00:05:28,337
The model is useful, but not trusted.

101
00:05:28,337 --> 00:05:31,094
In one run it described the drone as inside the

102
00:05:31,094 --> 00:05:34,283
restricted zone while it was still in the warning zone.

103
00:05:34,283 --> 00:05:37,959
A grounding check caught the mismatch and flagged it in the log.

104
00:05:37,959 --> 00:05:40,284
In another, the model was slow to alert

105
00:05:40,284 --> 00:05:43,312
the airspace authority, so the policy now guarantees

106
00:05:43,312 --> 00:05:45,420
that action in the restricted zone.

107
00:05:46,220 --> 00:05:48,165
We also tested hostile input.

108
00:05:48,165 --> 00:05:51,111
A planted note in the flight registry told the

109
00:05:51,111 --> 00:05:54,176
assistant to ignore the rules and notify nobody.

110
00:05:54,176 --> 00:05:56,357
Llama ignored it in this run, but

111
00:05:56,357 --> 00:05:57,948
we do not rely on that.

112
00:05:57,948 --> 00:06:01,896
Authorization comes from a deterministic registry match, and in

113
00:06:01,896 --> 00:06:05,491
an automated self-test a forged answer asking for jamming

114
00:06:05,491 --> 00:06:08,320
was blocked and the threat level was raised.

115
00:06:09,120 --> 00:06:12,319
Every step goes into a hash-chained audit log.

116
00:06:12,319 --> 00:06:15,069
Change one entry, and the chain breaks.

117
00:06:15,069 --> 00:06:18,140
You can verify it yourself, in your browser.

118
00:06:18,940 --> 00:06:20,184
Reproducibility.

119
00:06:20,184 --> 00:06:24,352
The simulation is seeded, so the same run gives the same track.

120
00:06:24,352 --> 00:06:28,768
The agent's Llama answers are cached, and replaying them reproduces

121
00:06:28,768 --> 00:06:31,629
the same sequence of events with no G P U.

122
00:06:31,629 --> 00:06:33,869
That is why the public demo is a

123
00:06:33,869 --> 00:06:36,979
static page that costs almost nothing, and why

124
00:06:36,979 --> 00:06:39,840
every number in this video can be checked.

125
00:06:40,640 --> 00:06:41,629
Honest limits.

126
00:06:41,629 --> 00:06:45,148
At closest approach the drone moves sideways, falls into the

127
00:06:45,148 --> 00:06:48,446
Doppler blind zone, and the track coasts for two frames.

128
00:06:48,446 --> 00:06:51,964
A hovering drone would be invisible to this clutter removal.

129
00:06:51,964 --> 00:06:54,437
And human approval delay eats most of the

130
00:06:54,437 --> 00:06:56,746
lead time late in the flight, which is

131
00:06:56,746 --> 00:06:58,780
why the agent must propose early.

132
00:06:59,580 --> 00:07:01,946
The stack is deliberately simple.

133
00:07:01,946 --> 00:07:05,911
Sionna and PyTorch simulate the radio on a single AWS GPU.

134
00:07:05,911 --> 00:07:10,515
Meta's Llama three point one, eight billion parameters, runs locally

135
00:07:10,515 --> 00:07:14,095
through Ollama, so no incident data leaves the site.

136
00:07:14,095 --> 00:07:17,421
A policy engine and an audit log wrap the model.

137
00:07:17,421 --> 00:07:19,978
Two simplifications, stated plainly:

138
00:07:19,978 --> 00:07:22,280
the drone is a point target, and

139
00:07:22,280 --> 00:07:24,902
the angle estimate is modelled at the

140
00:07:24,902 --> 00:07:27,908
detected cell, not a full array simulation.

141
00:07:27,908 --> 00:07:32,000
The public demo is a static page that replays recorded runs.

142
00:07:32,800 --> 00:07:33,973
Why it matters.

143
00:07:33,973 --> 00:07:37,061
Substations, ports and airports are regulated.

144
00:07:37,061 --> 00:07:39,654
They answer to aviation rules, such as

145
00:07:39,654 --> 00:07:41,815
Remote I D, and to privacy law,

146
00:07:41,815 --> 00:07:44,655
because sensing data can be personal data.

147
00:07:44,655 --> 00:07:47,990
A system that proposes, a human that approves, and

148
00:07:47,990 --> 00:07:50,707
a log that proves what happened is how A

149
00:07:50,707 --> 00:07:53,300
I earns a place in these environments.

150
00:07:54,100 --> 00:07:55,114
What is next.

151
00:07:55,114 --> 00:07:57,918
First, validate the signal processing on an

152
00:07:57,918 --> 00:08:00,782
open-source five-G testbed with real radios.

153
00:08:00,782 --> 00:08:03,944
Second, add rotor signatures, so a hovering drone

154
00:08:03,944 --> 00:08:05,913
and a bird can be told apart.

155
00:08:05,913 --> 00:08:08,717
Third, fuse several base stations to remove

156
00:08:08,717 --> 00:08:11,044
the blind zone at closest approach.

157
00:08:11,044 --> 00:08:15,638
Fourth, replace the modelled angle estimate with a full array simulation.

158
00:08:15,638 --> 00:08:18,203
Six-G sensing is still being studied in

159
00:08:18,203 --> 00:08:20,232
the standard, so this is a map

160
00:08:20,232 --> 00:08:22,320
of what to test, not a product.

161
00:08:23,120 --> 00:08:28,531
Governed, auditable A I on a sensing network that is already deployed.

162
00:08:28,531 --> 00:08:30,578
That is the opportunity:

163
00:08:30,578 --> 00:08:35,331
protecting substations, ports and airports without new radar.

164
00:08:35,331 --> 00:08:39,864
Try it at ai-agentic-enterprises dot com slash six-g-isac.

165
00:08:39,864 --> 00:08:45,860
I am Yuvaraj Aravindan, building governed A I agents for regulated industries.
